---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Image credits">
  <section class="container simple-page">
    <p class="label">Image credits</p>
    <h1>Image credits</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The Talosaur team edited the photos, graphics and the homepage video on this site from the sources below.</p>
      <p>Page headers, big photo bands, inset photos and Home page photos: video and photos from Pexels contributors (free to use under the Pexels license), including Joerg Mangelsen (ocean sunfish), ekamelev (bubble anemone) and Kindel Media (fish eye), and NOAA Ocean Exploration (public domain). The nature photos have false-colour edits.</p>
      <p>Page header photos on Who we are, News, Work with us and Positions (zoanthids, porcelain crab, soft coral, pygmy seahorse): [Photographer and source for these four photos.] Shown inverted (as negatives), some only in part.</p>
      <p>Animals in the depth graphic on The Problem page: Tracey Jennings / Ocean Image Bank (squid), Gregory Piper / Ocean Image Bank (jellyfish), Mike Bartick / Ocean Image Bank (larval fish) and Richard Barnden / Ocean Image Bank (two drifting animals), used for non-commercial purposes. The squid and jellyfish have colour edits. The research ship there is our own drawing.</p>
      <p>Turtle engraving in the homepage video: David Bushnell's Turtle submarine (1775), public domain. The green Turtle drawing on the Home page is the team's own.</p>
      <p>Colour-edited accents: the radiolarian on the Home page is from Ernst Haeckel's Radiolaria plates for the HMS Challenger report (1887); the fish skeleton on the Who we are page is from a 1900 book plate (Internet Archive Book Images). Both are public domain. The seafloor texture behind Latest news on the Home page is a NOAA Ocean Exploration multibeam sonar point cloud (Deepwater Wonders of Wake, 2016), and the animals on the Donate and News pages are from NOAA Ocean Exploration dive video (Mona Canyon, 2018; expedition EX2206, 2022; American Samoa, 2026), all public domain. The jellyfish and the ship on the Work with us page are from Pexels videos (Pexels license).</p>
      <p>Photos in the content sections (At a glance and Habitat on The Problem, Who we work with, Where it goes, Life on the team, Where we test on News): NOAA Ocean Exploration dive video (public domain) and Pexels videos (Pexels license). The map on The Problem page is drawn from GSHHG coastline data (Wessel and Smith).</p>
      <p>Homepage video: footage from Pexels and NOAA Ocean Exploration, with historical illustrations in the public domain.</p>
      <!-- PLACEHOLDER: list the remaining third-party stills in the homepage video, with the creator and licence -->
      <p>[Credits for the remaining third-party stills in the homepage video.]</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .simple-page {
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  h1 {
    margin-block: var(--space-sm) var(--space-lg);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 60ch;
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .prose p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
</style>
